#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf30a48-c9e1-56ae-a6e7-d1c98a8ff3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529217} {:answer/id #uuid "c73dcc5f-2dfd-545e-8686-1192c87baf69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529217} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c836807-d3ab-5654-828e-8cfc6899a48e" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529226} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3352e3a8-a31e-5489-b928-3600c98b31be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529215} {:answer/id #uuid "7eea3389-ae38-5700-acdf-02838bc43de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529233} :answer-field/id #uuid "7319f1b7-7a37-5090-bc1d-79d9eee3c8d9" :answer-field/key "$det(Q)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529231} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59fb7559-abec-5a5a-8f38-66e0e05169a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529221} {:answer/id #uuid "edd1215a-893f-52eb-9b40-de5e574e65b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529249} :answer-field/id #uuid "885ed53f-bd5b-5462-9002-72b92ffeea39" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0f86b247-8f09-549d-8811-1a1520e150f7" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}- \\sqrt{2})+1(- \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+4 \\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+1\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{20}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$Q$ is a singular matrix” and $det(Q)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "623acce1-fff7-50e6-838b-7b0788c7f4cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529223} {:answer/id #uuid "6f989a4f-56c1-50c2-8a6c-3fc3965f7ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529223} :answer-field/id #uuid "25dfb153-bf6c-5bc8-81b3-3f8c8e55fd75" :answer-field/key "$R$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529212} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ea2a503-d065-55b2-8fd1-d25f4f09f9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529227} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8e8b31-aab5-5ebf-9a6d-c0ce4e4dec83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529227} :answer-field/id #uuid "7227b94e-e860-5cf3-bd06-90e04b0b9867" :answer-field/key "$R$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529230} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0849452b-707f-5059-a47a-da630beb226a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529208} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6b1114-f6aa-5ce4-b2fd-bd99b174c462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529208} :answer-field/id #uuid "e852e547-0a05-5456-8b75-a7942f7d0613" :answer-field/key "$det(R)=\\frac{4}{5} - \\sqrt{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2eb19151-d434-5c12-a703-9c37eed13782" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{4})+(- \\sqrt{2})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (- \\sqrt{2})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\cdot \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} \\\\  & =1- \\frac{7}{10}- \\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =- \\frac{13}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(R)=- \\frac{13}{10}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$R$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f10ee21-6b89-5e0f-a04a-e49bcfa4c907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529218} {:answer/id #uuid "fe16b8a0-13d3-5554-8edd-ff60470da1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529255} :answer-field/id #uuid "283cd5ed-404d-513a-8b29-74966f63a5de" :answer-field/key "$D$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bbf7977-41f6-5bb2-bc84-9b91b4aa2e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529239} {:answer/id #uuid "b655c0ce-1985-5548-8848-5fb9f83b43c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529239} :answer-field/id #uuid "57424796-2385-5961-a89c-2d74ee068fcd" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "670467a6-e81c-5c99-be97-28f089cc9d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529225} {:answer/id #uuid "db4c2f01-affa-593d-9e86-c0393439ae7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529247} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbad6dc2-1046-5c88-93da-58012356da43" :answer-field/key "$D$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "58253580-dd5c-51ba-b42f-aea83256773d" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\frac{12 \\sqrt{2}}{25}- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}+\\frac{3}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{6}{25}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87861eb3-5f89-51bb-a83b-a2a2f34426b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529234} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3191b2-823c-5ff2-9063-c65501bb4dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529252} :answer-field/id #uuid "0187374c-e9df-5d8a-b2e7-160b085f7e8a" :answer-field/key "$D$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529206} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e9331ae-3672-5960-a137-fec2b5ed94e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529222} {:answer/id #uuid "c78e9128-09e7-5dbd-8651-62e753136ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529245} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0b79b6-ad2c-596a-b49a-2f831ec2b99d" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529236} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f014eef-cc68-5016-a321-04739726f29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529237} {:answer/id #uuid "9792a9f2-799d-58ba-9247-36ec66135b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529238} :answer-field/id #uuid "a28fbb07-9011-5ca0-97ac-fffd6b36f2ee" :answer-field/key "$D$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "71433954-37ca-5812-b277-05c8f0f0a4e4" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{8})+(-1)(- \\frac{7}{5}+\\frac{21}{20})+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}+(-1)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{20}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{40}+\\frac{7}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e93d6b-04f6-5d4f-b5b6-d410c07da27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529210} {:answer/id #uuid "ac318550-2f86-5de9-9b06-3ca0446a83ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529210} :answer-field/id #uuid "c469465a-d12b-5568-8d4c-567bbab54c8b" :answer-field/key "$det(N)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48843694-108e-5d14-bd01-367c88bbda7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529216} {:answer/id #uuid "66105f24-eeac-5dfa-906a-b29c7b525374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529216} :answer-field/id #uuid "caa6403a-374c-56a6-9e6e-eff87afdb677" :answer-field/key "$N$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529246} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0634ee5a-1fe4-5615-bb1f-de6045b61f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529207} {:answer/id #uuid "25008e01-79c7-58b7-aeed-e63a6324cfd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529211} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd12a97e-9c51-5ed4-8b7d-226953a0be67" :answer-field/key "$N$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f4817fa1-7160-5d08-adc7-564c3155c399" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 1 & 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 1 & 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2})+(-1)(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+2 \\sqrt{2}(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{10 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{9}- \\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$N$ is a singular matrix” and $det(N)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304925d3-7e53-5fe7-8e1e-d1a597888552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\left(-10{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530653} {:answer/id #uuid "654e7f30-de76-5ae3-8bbd-6b7df7e6bc6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\left(-10{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530655} {:answer/id #uuid "982b1685-d000-58c5-ba0a-ec034ddd1e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\left(4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530656} {:answer/id #uuid "d3614844-c34f-5565-8b62-a5cdc15b209c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\left(4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530657} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d5f3ae-eda8-59c2-bfc9-812725102f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\left(-5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530658} :answer-field/id #uuid "4db95f3e-81e0-5b82-b974-5027285c9250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "06e31152-0f29-5cdd-9766-cb494216c61b" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b693165c8f6201e96f0a972746324c65a308d1f143f229c465b6aa22b7aeb16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5\\left((x-y)\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=400,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(5,0),$ as shown above?\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5\\left(x-y\\right)$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\left(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5\\left({\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =5\\left(-5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dea8cf4-abca-5534-a7f2-29d2bbc6488c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530739} {:answer/id #uuid "2b16d35f-0ee6-51e5-baa1-4b69ca4200ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530746} {:answer/id #uuid "542a3357-432b-562c-913e-663a74e70ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530753} {:answer/id #uuid "998816b6-609b-5a13-94bc-6d1fc0091e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530762} {:answer/id #uuid "f15f424a-8343-5761-8e46-b77b237d01af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530776}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530776} :answer-field/id #uuid "206b27c4-d78b-5d3d-a971-63758f948d29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c0d33b2-47e5-55ae-b760-568401b27bca" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =- y, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =- x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(- \\left(0\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "187aaf9a-23e2-5249-9777-13f85fb382b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530742} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0d9f94-0a0a-5915-9dee-44c0efe84a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530744} {:answer/id #uuid "64249498-a798-535b-9188-cb51e727cda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530758} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbf1197-1026-5682-934d-702aeaecd9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530766} {:answer/id #uuid "fb022fd9-b223-5e5f-8dd8-df9398c24466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530779} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5939e5b-1a1e-5cfa-9cdb-592cf05e8314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d5a26a2-4d59-5e5b-a1a3-1666d9c373b2" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t + 1,t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(x\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + y^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 1,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 2,2\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(1\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2+4- \\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f421d3-0ff0-50e3-990e-8e56151c2e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530748} {:answer/id #uuid "56eb8950-e15e-52f0-ae16-fb7586994618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530754} {:answer/id #uuid "8e134253-f04d-5625-a0ee-1441bd7c69d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530760} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9b8357-5dc4-52c7-bb8c-ef2afae597fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530770} {:answer/id #uuid "da268a2e-44cb-5d08-8af4-2fcd394f38bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530774} :answer-field/id #uuid "639fe49f-92ca-5726-bea7-608bd05da2a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5742e24a-185d-570b-8c93-c58d8a73320b" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =- 3 y, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =- 3 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 x y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(- 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right)-\\left(- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "373cde8b-2ba1-502e-9e79-9a586dd3b83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1 - e^{\\pi^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530749} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfec755-6997-5f15-9fed-98e9c1c3f129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 - 4 e^{\\pi^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530750} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b7102d-f815-5dd3-8623-99aa71c67037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2 + 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530763} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fc0238-69de-524e-b760-febf67f8aa31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 - 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530773} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3fe867-4ad2-521d-9672-37b784918df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530773} :answer-field/id #uuid "0168e73e-a498-5adc-add3-09c8a7057194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bc21e937-2785-5c98-a85b-95973fde2ff7" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2 y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(\\pi) & =\\langle \\pi,\\pi^{2}\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi,\\pi^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi,\\pi^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(0\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0- 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}\\right)-\\left(0-2\\right) \\\\  & =2 - 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ece7d5e-e40b-5322-bfbd-10fda82d3db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530740} {:answer/id #uuid "4852ea1f-8754-5a01-89c0-e3f5916b4530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530751} {:answer/id #uuid "5a844f1c-aba0-5582-9d00-7703dff22261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530756} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f71ec9-9847-53ee-b0ad-cc4c31b80a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530771} {:answer/id #uuid "f51b6044-fe39-551e-9675-ce6e2d45770d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530740} :answer-field/id #uuid "c58ed758-635e-5ca0-8b27-580bc7221389" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c2766a0b-7719-5fca-8223-c9d01bd4c6c3" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t - 2,t - 2\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =3 x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =3 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{3 y^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle -2,-2\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle -1,-1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(-1,-1)-f(-2,-2) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}-6-6 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f293979-2708-5915-879f-2d1960a8af31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530741} {:answer/id #uuid "19d51678-611e-5f6b-8d6d-d48198928a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530743} {:answer/id #uuid "7985ce09-6e30-561e-a33e-cc009dbcd028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530759} {:answer/id #uuid "97a9befc-e3ed-5fa8-9702-f38ae767d815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530761} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e07c78-c2b2-599d-8262-a8c06b58e4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530772}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530761} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9adcd3d-8721-5ea1-92cd-e467acbfd7cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9033e6e-7d03-5cf3-9e4f-e08eff1e5a07" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(3 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =3 x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33802d27-dd89-5a46-aaaf-65bd2ba0847d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2637}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530793} {:answer/id #uuid "938126e1-749e-5ced-9bd7-f58a219e4aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530818} {:answer/id #uuid "c20a51e1-07b3-52de-a60a-f1119be7ae39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{293}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530827} {:answer/id #uuid "e52a06ff-7083-59c8-93fa-6b556c31ac82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{293}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530841} {:answer/id #uuid "e6422438-4494-58a7-8306-ef2b2c61dc7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{462}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530843}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530827} :answer-field/id #uuid "686b4b88-2dcc-5887-a216-28891d2ec098" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1a3aec82-ec8f-58f2-b9b2-fdff35886aa6" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-4,-3,-4)$, $B(-9,1,-10)$, $C(-5,-5,-11).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-9i+j-10k)-(-4i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-5i+4j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-5,4,-6⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-5i-5j-11k)-(-4i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-i-2j-7k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -7\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-7)-(-6)\\cdot (-2))i-((-5)\\cdot (-7)-(-6)\\cdot (-1))j+((-5)\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-40i-29j+14k \\\\  & =⟨-40,-29,14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-40)^{2}+(-29)^{2}+{14}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2637} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{293}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{293}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086b28b8-2f00-5cb7-9a9b-33a0bb35a463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530782} {:answer/id #uuid "3b685097-5fff-5c5d-9a28-b20462013563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530797} {:answer/id #uuid "9978e296-f953-52ab-bd26-da7dae9ae604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530820} {:answer/id #uuid "c7935a35-d82a-55ef-a5a3-1ac807b65b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530831} {:answer/id #uuid "f8083c90-dd25-532f-a683-fbef59b2240d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530797} :answer-field/id #uuid "00b5a4a1-037c-5c23-ab2f-91d5e2368598" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1fc0d6d3-68d3-53c1-9126-183c39fa054f" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-1,4,-2)$, $B(0,2,-2)$, $C(-1,2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2j-2k)-(-i+4j-2k) \\\\  & =i-2j \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+2j-3k)-(-i+4j-2k) \\\\  & =-2j-k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =2i+j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f716af9-0180-5b87-b139-f9ae40fa13c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3493}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530788} {:answer/id #uuid "370af184-fb4c-5e45-b1c5-eae2346255f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3493}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530795} {:answer/id #uuid "472aa6f2-32cd-5acd-ad50-82088699ad78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3782}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530800} {:answer/id #uuid "8b473751-ee49-5695-a457-bf5814e251a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530815} {:answer/id #uuid "f1695c5c-320b-50fe-afa7-145f77bb48b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3493}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530845}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530845} :answer-field/id #uuid "60b36088-d013-5647-abad-85f30beff231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2dde6928-3f9f-5db5-a31c-0089fe841550" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(4,-2,3)$, $B(5,-7,-3)$, $C(8,-5,9).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(5i-7j-3k)-(4i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =i-5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨1,-5,-6⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(8i-5j+9k)-(4i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =4i-3j+6k \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot 6-(-6)\\cdot (-3))i-(1\\cdot 6-(-6)\\cdot 4)j+(1\\cdot (-3)-(-5)\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =-48i-30j+17k \\\\  & =⟨-48,-30,17⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-48)^{2}+(-30)^{2}+{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3493}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{3493}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "593b334b-7c4a-5792-a874-fbba73e76ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530803} {:answer/id #uuid "81c11d00-99ec-5fc2-8326-c4f24360e1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530813} {:answer/id #uuid "bce57cfe-b0a7-5c3c-a53c-813a491136b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530825} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1ae519-3702-5cae-86f0-03ade164257d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530832} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c2c279-91e9-5ca7-8d7e-f47097d3cd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530803} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15b0211-dbf2-59de-91d0-67f26a64e9b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74e78994-81c8-507d-80d7-7417a66c3784" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(0,-4,1)$, $B(-1,-5,1)$, $C(0,-2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-i-5j+k)-(-4j+k) \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2j+3k)-(-4j+k) \\\\  & =2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)i-((-1)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0)j+((-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i+2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "923ddb0a-12de-5c56-8ffd-3e5a4611916a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2613}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530817} {:answer/id #uuid "a8577cd6-5f0e-5342-a44f-5f3b2ab072ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2613}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530823} {:answer/id #uuid "d9dc805e-c764-5051-99c2-9efdcadf13fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2613}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530835} {:answer/id #uuid "e401188e-3b6b-59f6-b120-38f9d52a3514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530838} {:answer/id #uuid "e57f1b1e-4cb0-5b42-b369-e443920b1d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2622}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530817} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7b1ce8-d286-5955-8bab-c314598ae3c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "79b0b84f-e3d1-59ef-bee0-590d040d6e16" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(3,-2,4)$, $B(10,0,0)$, $C(6,-7,6).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(10i)-(3i-2j+4k) \\\\  & =7i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨7,2,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(6i-7j+6k)-(3i-2j+4k) \\\\  & =3i-5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨3,-5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 7 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-5))i-(7\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 3)j+(7\\cdot (-5)-2\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-16i-26j-41k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-26,-41⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-16)^{2}+(-26)^{2}+(-41)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2613}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2613}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d88d5b-ce0e-5f1d-83e8-14d31db29346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530784} {:answer/id #uuid "56bbec4c-fba7-5b9f-84f8-58792e66d75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530801} {:answer/id #uuid "5816e6f4-de9c-59d6-8e64-30b6a7fee35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530802} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb079d0-9f17-58d4-bed1-252323dc5e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530807} {:answer/id #uuid "a82e5757-4147-5fc3-bb91-faea8ed642f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530807} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fe13558-c04f-55d0-9b8b-2599812b93b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "961ffd7b-3ddc-5e8e-8154-c1d2c7260e33" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-2,-2,-3)$, $B(-1,-2,-1)$, $C(-2,-4,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-i-2j-k)-(-2i-2j-3k) \\\\  & =i+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i-4j-2k)-(-2i-2j-3k) \\\\  & =-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =4i-j-2k \\\\  & =⟨4,-1,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aba964f-912f-5c11-ba41-4f08d7d851a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530785} {:answer/id #uuid "39fca8c4-ffab-5662-910d-94763a8cdf68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "141" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530796} {:answer/id #uuid "8250c0d9-3360-5081-ba97-c5e4c1e87cc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{282}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530814} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f89c75-d031-5f54-af9b-d8223a72b32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{682}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530828} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e66024-ae9f-5633-8bd0-2982e94b368b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{282}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530814} :answer-field/id #uuid "59fe9a3c-a36a-5ec8-805d-3b2dfd1716c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c090616c-dc00-5125-b106-d8e2aee23a6a" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(2,0,1)$, $B(3,-1,-2)$, $C(-1,-2,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i-j-2k)-(2i+k) \\\\  & =i-j-3k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i-2j-6k)-(2i+k) \\\\  & =-3i-2j-7k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -7\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-7)-(-3)\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot (-7)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+(1\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =i+16j-5k \\\\  & =⟨1,16,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{16}^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{282}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{282}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239f428d-4367-5f63-8950-0dae6f9df5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530791} {:answer/id #uuid "443c0fc9-7bb8-5490-a6e4-cec9131ff874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530799} {:answer/id #uuid "903741d0-c5b8-5064-b537-e97b4756f7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530816} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b73253-2ed1-5370-abd6-23e85a5d1865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530829} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b04ded-ff47-5514-af3c-93899d51f709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530837} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f61f576-5bff-5529-9f8b-fe2c718e22ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4387d5f-11af-570b-b2c5-44f500581664" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(1,2,-2)$, $B(3,2,-2)$, $C(2,2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+2j-2k)-(i+2j-2k) \\\\  & =2i \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+2j)-(i+2j-2k) \\\\  & =i+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)j+(2\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-4j \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a4d44ac-7a04-53af-883c-d84299462108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530806} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb14737-a4dc-5c33-8d76-db1413b3c0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530810} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bb03c8-6969-593a-bebd-f73b084888a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530834} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4f54f7-762f-559c-b251-2e94eff699ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530836} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd39de1-2821-5e1a-ac29-859d1dc8b192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530836} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7356952-eb01-5c8f-ad3e-2396e474424e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea1dd373-8a23-58ba-959d-745d87d1958b" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(2,1,-2)$, $B(0,0,-2)$, $C(2,3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2k)-(2i+j-2k) \\\\  & =-2i-j \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j)-(2i+j-2k) \\\\  & =2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)i-((-2)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i+4j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c91fca4-1a47-542b-92ef-c0df31262767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530787} {:answer/id #uuid "3421f176-ac70-50d9-9c98-b8851842557d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{705}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530794} {:answer/id #uuid "3c255ebd-4d34-5ced-b13a-f051713a6fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{705}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530798} {:answer/id #uuid "59f097ce-80c9-5adc-98ab-810d7b4e40b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{705}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530804} {:answer/id #uuid "e4aceeab-78d8-50bf-9f5d-807f902ca96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1189}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530840}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530794} :answer-field/id #uuid "0996cec4-943d-5dc2-aad8-fb7a8fd031b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f8041b05-a284-546b-8ef4-48d196f31e3d" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-3,-3,-4)$, $B(-6,1,-6)$, $C(-5,-2,-10).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-6i+j-6k)-(-3i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-3i+4j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,4,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-5i-2j-10k)-(-3i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot 1)i-((-3)\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+((-3)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-22i-14j+5k \\\\  & =⟨-22,-14,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-22)^{2}+(-14)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{705}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{705}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed89854-ce24-5e33-9d9f-77633ee05901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531805} {:answer/id #uuid "5435d236-631f-5cf6-a1af-7c24ebc30e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531816} {:answer/id #uuid "585b87b5-9e11-5026-936e-4e61825b1445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-224" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531817} {:answer/id #uuid "be0db405-a911-5d94-9f80-93c89ee42be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531830} {:answer/id #uuid "c970e40f-e018-5931-8bb7-7afdd50e22cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "224" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531831} :answer-field/id #uuid "a843646c-3bd8-57f7-b6c7-b5130883f76b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a372134-3f0d-5612-aa85-09775257279c" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v,\\,u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-1\\le v\\le 3}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\nF(r(u,v))=⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩=5 u + 6 v + 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(5 u + 6 v + 3)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}[v \\left(5 u + 3 v + 3\\right)]_{v=-1}^{v=3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}(20 u + 36)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =[2 u \\left(5 u + 18\\right)]_{-1}^{3} \\\\ & =224.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d3bf91-1b9e-50e1-b487-002e4121a6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531804} {:answer/id #uuid "42225fe9-ade9-5b18-9132-55f036dc3f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531813} {:answer/id #uuid "b4be5f22-1fbe-5fac-ba0f-a34045b52bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1092" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531828} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ba4c1a-4db7-52c0-a20a-62aa2d4a4381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531829} {:answer/id #uuid "f13f373d-207a-56fa-99a9-a1ac19de5e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531804} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f9f5640-40e0-5f7c-a1ff-41e54d4e16f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "326926db-5725-5b57-8003-30f866bb2f70" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v,\\,u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 3}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\nF(r(u,v))=⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩=5 u + 6 v + 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(5 u + 6 v + 3)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}[v \\left(5 u + 3 v + 3\\right)]_{v=-1}^{v=3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}(20 u + 36)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =[2 u \\left(5 u + 18\\right)]_{-2}^{1} \\\\ & =78.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01cb0663-140c-55be-a037-3632d1f8ad3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531802} {:answer/id #uuid "21032641-051e-5d4d-80d1-e40cdd7891b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531809} {:answer/id #uuid "51c051c6-3526-5041-b89e-19c85bcb555e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1792" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531815} {:answer/id #uuid "dc46265a-5d8a-51ce-82ba-a5675e274c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "232" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531834} {:answer/id #uuid "e1628a2d-7339-59ec-a5d5-b85e611c7df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{122}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531809} :answer-field/id #uuid "88ff5813-50ae-5cf5-8858-de04c9c1b633" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ee8b4d4-16bf-53a3-a6a2-18b752c2a7e0" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩\\cd